 "capability": "chromosomal-sex",
 "title": "Chromosomal sex per cell",
 "conclusion": "On 171 samples with sex in their GEO metadata (472,363 cells), Singlet labels 97.0% of cells with their sample's recorded sex, against 68.0% for cellXY, 50.6% for xist-chrY-rule.",
 "methods": "Data: the geo_sex21 cohort row, 21 studies whose GEO sample characteristics record donor sex, with both sexes in each study. Counts are Singlet's served gene counts (exon + intron) of the called cells. Every tool sees the same cells and the same counts. Truth: the sex recorded in GEO. Singlet: singlet.celllabels.sex. It uses the split of sex-informative UMIs (XIST against 10 non-PAR chrY genes) as a per-cell likelihood ratio. Cells are pooled per donor (per sample when genotype demultiplexing found one donor), and the male fraction is fitted by EM. cellXY::classifySex (phipsonlab, GitHub 0.99.0) runs with defaults (qc = TRUE) on the full count matrix, in random blocks of at least 5,000 cells because it densifies the matrix. The XIST/chrY rule calls female when XIST > 0 and no MSY UMI, male when MSY > 0 and XIST = 0, and makes no call otherwise. A tool's sample call is the majority of its labelled cells; Singlet's is the donor call. Per-cell metrics count a missing label as wrong (accuracy) or leave the cell out (precision). 95% CIs are bootstrap over samples.",
 "status": "draft",
 "generated_at": "2026-10-25T15:52:47+00:00",
 "owner": "bench-public",
 "singlet": {
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "commit": "bf1f5741144eb845eddec94f0417931c4533e98e",
